"""Menga on Kaggle: generate the generation queue's clips with Wan 2.2 14B on T4 GPUs.

This file is the Kaggle kernel itself (a script kernel, GPU and internet on).
`python -m menga kaggle export` copies it into a push folder with the job list
written into JOBS_JSON below; it also reads a jobs.json placed next to it.
Nothing here is imported by the build: the repo's tests import the pure
helpers (sizes, frame counts, the sampler split, the ComfyUI graphs).

What it runs for clips (pinned below, nothing taken from "latest"; the image-edit models: see further down):

- ComfyUI (a pinned release) with ComfyUI-GGUF (a pinned commit), headless,
  driven through its HTTP API: one ComfyUI per GPU (T4 x2: two clips at once).
- Wan 2.2 I2V A14B as GGUF (high-noise and low-noise experts), the umt5-xxl
  text encoder, the Wan 2.1 VAE and the lightx2v step-distill LoRA, the same
  LoRA and strengths (3 on the high-noise expert, 1 on the low-noise one), cfg 1,
  sizes and frame counts as the two HF ZeroGPU Spaces the pilot used, so a
  clip asks for the same thing whichever backend makes it:
    i2v              zerogpu-aoti/wan2-2-fp8da-aoti-faster: crop to 832x480-ish, UniPC, shift 3
    first-last       mcp-tools/wan-2-2-first-last-frame: fit to 832 / 480, Euler, shift 8
    anchored         first-last with the same still as first and last frame
  The two experts switch where the model's boundary (0.9 of the noise) says.
- T4 RAM is the limit (both experts do not fit at once), so the clips run in
  two phases: every job's high-noise steps (latents saved), the models freed,
  then every job's low-noise steps and the VAE decode. Since v5 the GPUs share
  one list of steps (stills and latents in one input folder), so any GPU takes
  any job's next step.
- Surviving the infrastructure (v5; run 2026-11-08-night lost 17 of 19 clips):
  the host's RAM (~29 GB) barely holds two servers, and ComfyUI was killed
  while loading models, never with a Python error. So: a host-wide gate lets
  one graph at a time load its models (until its sampler shows progress); a
  server that dies or stops answering in any phase is restarted and the step
  tried again, up to MAX_TRIES per job (no limit per server); a job that runs
  out of tries goes back to the queue (error_kind server or timeout: not an
  attempt); a server that will not restart gives its GPU up and the other
  takes its jobs; after SOLO_DEATHS deaths within SOLO_WINDOW one server is
  stopped for good (one ComfyUI on the host). No step starts that cannot end
  before the run's hours (jobs.json `hours`, else Kaggle's 12 h cap); those jobs
  come back with error_kind session (not an attempt). run.log and the manifest
  record the host's free RAM per step and each server's exit code when it dies.

A B pair's intermediate keyframe (cell-v2 19, mode `image-edit`) is an image
edit with Qwen-Image-Edit-2509 as GGUF (Q4_K_M, ~13 GB, fits a T4 with ComfyUI
offloading the rest), its Qwen2.5-VL text encoder and its VAE, in the same
ComfyUI (TextEncodeQwenImageEditPlus: both stills go in as Picture 1 and
Picture 2, so the model sees where every character is in both drawings). The
latent it starts from is the two stills blended half and half (`init: blend`;
`first`: the first still alone), re-drawn by `denoise` of the way, at the
keyframe canvas size, cfg as the job says, Euler / simple. Its model files are
fetched only when the run has edit jobs, and the edits run first, on GPU 0
alone (the 20B model's text encoder and the Wan experts never share the host's
RAM); then the edit files are deleted and the clips run exactly as without
them. If the edit models cannot be fetched, the edit jobs are recorded with
error_kind "setup" (never reached the model: not an attempt) and the clips
still run. The edit repos are not pinned to a commit yet: the run resolves
each to the commit it fetched and records it in manifest.json
(`edit_models`); pin those revisions here after the first good run.

Output (/kaggle/working, what `kaggle kernels output` downloads):
    <clip>.mp4          one per job that made a clip (16 fps, H.264), named by its clip name
    <clip>.png          one per image-edit job that made an intermediate keyframe (the canvas size)
    manifest.json       what ran: versions, models, GPUs, setup seconds, and per job its
                        inputs, outcome (ok and file, or error and error_kind: input when the
                        still never reached the model; server, timeout or session when it was
                        lost to the infrastructure; generation for a real error), seconds on
                        its GPU, steps lost and retried, lowest host RAM, and when it finished
    run.log             this script's log
"""

